<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- Is this a screen wide enough for the tablet treatment?
         ...
         Declared as a RESOURCE rather than computed in Kotlin so that code and
         layout cannot disagree. The wide layouts are selected by the -w840dp
         qualifier; a hand-rolled `isTablet()` reading smallestScreenWidthDp
         would be a second, independent definition of the same thing, and the
         two drift the moment one is changed — the classic symptom being a
         navigation rail on screen while the code still reasons about a bottom
         bar.

         WIDTH, not "is it a tablet". The question worth asking is how much
         horizontal room there is right now, which changes when a foldable
         opens, when a tablet is rotated, and in split-screen — where a tablet
         can legitimately be as narrow as a phone. `w840dp` follows the window,
         so all three cases answer correctly and for free.

         840dp, not 600dp: a 7-inch tablet in portrait is 600dp and the phone
         layout is still the right one there. -->
    <bool name="is_wide_screen">false</bool>
</resources>
